CI: off the deprecated Node 20 action runtime

The alpha.6 release succeeded, and correctly — prerelease flagged, the jar attached as
draco-2.0.0-alpha.6.jar with the right version in its name, and the written notes as the
release body rather than a commit list. But the run carried two deprecation annotations
that the local reading had missed:

  Node.js 20 is deprecated. actions/checkout@v4, actions/setup-java@v4 and
  softprops/action-gh-release@v2 target Node 20 and are being forced onto Node 24.

  setup-java v4 is deprecated and will no longer receive updates.

All three were left at the versions the workflow already had, on the judgement that they
were current. They were not, and not by a little: checkout is at v7, setup-java at v6, and
action-gh-release at v3. Bumped to those majors. `sbt/setup-sbt@v1` is current (v1.5.8
under the v1 tag) and is unchanged.

The inputs each action uses are stable across these majors — `distribution`,
`java-version`, `cache` on setup-java; `files`, `body_path`, `generate_release_notes`,
`prerelease`, `fail_on_unmatched_files` on the release action — so no configuration
changed with them.

THE HONEST CAVEAT, and it is the more interesting half. This change cannot be verified
until the next tag, because the release workflow is the only workflow and it only runs on
a tag. A repair to release infrastructure that is unexercised until the next release is
the same shape of problem as a CHANGELOG that waits for a release: the feedback arrives
at exactly the moment it is least welcome.

A push/PR workflow would fix that as a side effect. It would exercise checkout,
setup-java, setup-sbt and the suite on every push — three of these four bumps validated
immediately, and the fourth (the release step) is the only one that would genuinely have
to wait. That has been on the beta list already, for a separate reason: nothing currently
checks a commit until it is being released.

Not added here. It changes what happens on every push, which is Dev's call rather than a
side effect of a deprecation fix.
